      "final_message": "I rewrote `solution.py` so `parse_duration(s)` follows the README. Both tests in `python3 -m unittest -v test_public.py` pass.\n\n- **Parsing:** it uses `re.fullmatch` against `(?:([0-9]+)h)?(?:([0-9]+)m)?(?:([0-9]+)s)?`, which only accepts `h`, `m`, `s` in that order. Using `[0-9]` instead of `\\d` means only plain ASCII digits count.\n- **Result:** it returns total seconds as an int (`h*3600 + m*60 + s`). Leading zeros are fine, so `\"05m\"` gives 300.\n- **Errors:** non-string input raises `TypeError`. Anything else invalid raises `ValueError`: an empty string, wrong order, unknown units, missing digits, signs, spaces, a trailing newline, or non-ASCII digits.\n\nThe public tests only cover one valid and one invalid input. I also checked several of the other cases in a quick one-off script (not added to the test file), and they behaved as described.\n\nDONE: Rewrote parse_duration with an anchored regex for ordered h/m/s parts, returning total seconds and raising ValueError or TypeError on bad input; public tests pass.",
      "final_message_source": "stdout_response",
