      "final_message": "I added the `--dry-run` flag to `cli_tool.py` as the README describes, and it behaves correctly when run by hand.\n\n- **The change:** `--dry-run` is an argparse `store_true` flag that defaults to False. When it's given, the tool prints `DRY RUN: would {action} {path}` and stops before creating or deleting anything.\n- **Without the flag:** nothing changes. The tool still performs the action and prints `DONE: {action} {path}`.\n\nThere's no `test_public.py` in this directory, so there were no tests to run. Instead I ran the tool with a temporary file, `x.tmp`:\n- A dry-run create printed the expected line and left no file behind.\n- A real create made the file.\n- A dry-run delete left the file in place.\n- A real delete removed it.\n\nThe directory is back to just `README.md` and `cli_tool.py`.\n\nDONE: Added a --dry-run flag to cli_tool.py that prints \"DRY RUN: would {action} {path}\" without touching any files; normal behavior is unchanged.",
